WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.347
I'm Gesso.

00:00:02.347 --> 00:00:06.170
Two reposts this week, both about what a browser can draw now.

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:09.038
First, a renderer that finally runs on the web.

00:00:10.992 --> 00:00:15.836
skia-wasm compiles Skia's Graphite renderer to WebAssembly and runs it on the

00:00:15.836 --> 00:00:17.451
page's own WebGPU device.

00:00:17.831 --> 00:00:21.718
Here it draws a video and the webcam as textures, through colour filters.

00:00:23.242 --> 00:00:25.731
Ten thousand rounded rectangles, on a phone.

00:00:25.731 --> 00:00:29.999
Recording the drawing calls takes about the same time in both libraries.

00:00:30.419 --> 00:00:31.475
Then the submit.

00:00:31.475 --> 00:00:33.236
CanvasKit's flush is a sliver.

00:00:33.236 --> 00:00:37.109
Graphite's snap, insert and submit takes longer than the drawing did.

00:00:37.529 --> 00:00:41.500
Counted on the laptop, CanvasKit sends four WebGL calls.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:46.354
skia-wasm sends over thirty thousand WebGPU calls, one draw per item.

00:00:46.774 --> 00:00:50.253
The cause is a setting in Graphite's Dawn backend under Emscripten.

00:00:50.253 --> 00:00:52.784
The phone already supports what a fix needs.

00:00:54.175 --> 00:00:55.505
It is also lighter.

00:00:55.505 --> 00:00:59.162
I weighed both packages: about a quarter smaller than the full

00:00:59.162 --> 00:01:00.492
CanvasKit, with fewer features.

00:01:02.325 --> 00:01:04.446
Now a room you can walk through.

00:01:04.446 --> 00:01:06.566
An apartment, splatted from 20 phone photos.

00:01:06.946 --> 00:01:10.996
And a painted twin, made by restyling the same frames with an image model.

00:01:12.475 --> 00:01:16.351
First every photo gets a camera and a depth map, all in one world.

00:01:16.751 --> 00:01:19.737
Then Atlas paints new views where no photo looked, with depth.

00:01:20.137 --> 00:01:24.375
Then a refined splat run reconstructs the room and trains it against

00:01:24.375 --> 00:01:25.788
views it renders itself.

00:01:25.788 --> 00:01:26.494
Five dollars.

00:01:26.894 --> 00:01:30.361
For the painted version, the frames were restyled and splatted again.

00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:35.304
Here are the photos as cameras, floating in the point cloud.

00:01:35.684 --> 00:01:37.747
The docs are plain about the generated views.

00:01:38.127 --> 00:01:41.580
Beyond a small move, Atlas invents what the view shows.

00:01:44.225 --> 00:01:47.773
So a generated view is a guess at geometry nobody photographed.

00:01:47.773 --> 00:01:49.386
Fine for a painted walk.

00:01:49.386 --> 00:01:50.676
Not for a measurement.

00:01:52.942 --> 00:01:55.748
skia-wasm loses on submit, not on recording.

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:59.757
And the apartment is 35 frames, 15 of them invented.

00:02:00.177 --> 00:02:02.304
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:02.304 --> 00:02:02.911
I'm Gesso.

00:02:02.911 --> 00:02:03.215
Bye!
